window.EXOSTACK_INDEX=[{"t": "ExoStack: home", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "index.html", "x": "ExoStack is a modular and reconfigurable ROS 2 software framework for wearable robots: declarative ESP32 sensing and actuation hubs, configuration-driven nodes, human-in-the-loop personalization and a GUI."}, {"t": "How ExoStack works", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "overview.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "System overview", "p": "How ExoStack works", "u": "overview.html#system-overview", "x": "ExoStack has three modules that span the path from body-worn electronics to the assistance at each joint (Fig. 1 ). The Embedded Sensing and Actuation (ESA) module runs on ESP32 boards that serve as hubs over the lower limbs, the upper limbs, or both, connected to the ROS 2 graph by a micro-ROS agent over Wi-Fi or USB. On the host computer, the ExoCore module turns the hub data into gait events, assistance profiles, and actuator commands, and it closes the HIL loop when personalization is needed. Its nodes are created from YAML entries and run plug-ins selected by name, all kept in the folder exo_stack_resources , which the PASE module edits before launching the nodes. Because commands are ordinary ROS 2 topics, any actuator with a ROS 2 driver can also be used. Fig. 1 of the paper. ExoStack overview. ESA hubs worn on the body exchange sensor data and set-points with the ExoCore nodes through the micro-ROS agent. PASE writes the configuration, launches the nodes, and plots their topics. Each node instance is created from one configuration entry: ×N sensing and ×M actuation hubs, ×R ROS 2 drivers, ×E detectors, and ×P for each assisted motion (planner, profile builder, and, where configured, cost and HIL nodes)."}, {"t": "Embedded Sensing and Actuation (ESA)", "p": "How ExoStack works", "u": "overview.html#embedded-sensing-and-actuation-esa", "x": "Fig. 2 of the paper. ESA firmware of a hub. Text color marks when each step runs: drivers register before setup() (green), five calls bring the board up once (red), and every loop() (blue) packetizes the devices into msg.intensities[] and hands each actuator its latest command. publish() and actuate() spin the micro-ROS executor, which runs the publication timer and the sync and command callbacks; red and green arrows mark subscribed and published topics. Declaring a Hub ESA is an Arduino library that hides micro-ROS behind a few calls, so a hub is a short sketch. The sketch declares its devices in deviceList[] , one row per device with its interface type ( AI , DI , or I2C ), pin or address, label, and options such as a filter and its parameters, a pin mode, or an I²C bus ( Supplementary Table S1 ). Five calls in setup() then bring the board up (Fig. 2 ). setupCommunication(\"wifi\", ssid, pass, agent_ip, port) or setupCommunication(\"usb\") opens the link to the agent, and initializeSystem() configures the devices. Next, setupNode(name) , setupPublisher(topic, rate) , and one setupActuation(topic, type, out, x1, x2) per actuator declare the node and its interfaces, and bootSystem() creates all micro-ROS entities and arms a 3-s watchdog. Sensing and Actuation On a sensing hub, the loop calls readSensorsData() and publish(true) . Analog inputs are read and optionally filtered per channel by MOV_AVG , MEDIAN , EXPONENTIAL , or a second-order Butterworth section ( BUTTER2_LPF , BUTTER2_HPF , BUTTER2_BPF , BUTTER2_NOTCH ). Digital inputs are read in NORMAL , PDOWN , or PUP mode, a"}, {"t": "ExoCore", "p": "How ExoStack works", "u": "overview.html#exocore", "x": "Fig. 3 of the paper. ExoCore, the generic ROS 2 nodes, and their topics. ×E, ×P, ×C, and ×H count the instances created from YAML entries. Solid arrows carry ROS data, dotted arrows optional inputs, the dash-dotted loop the physical feedback through the wearer, and dashed arrows what is loaded at start. The candidate number k travels in the profile, status, and cost messages, so the optimizer accepts only the cost of the candidate it proposed. ExoCore is the ROS 2 package exo_stack (Fig. 3 ). Launch files start its six executables, sync , gait_event_detector , mplanner , actuation_profile , cost , and hil , once per entry of sensors.yaml , gait_events.yaml , mplanner.yaml , and profile.yaml . In a bilateral hip exosuit, for example, flexion- and extension-start events on both legs yield four detectors, each hip receives a planner and a profile node, and switching a profile to HIL mode adds a cost and an optimizer node, the ×C and ×H instances in Fig. 3 , at most one each per profile. Supplementary Table S3 and the documentation website list the topics that connect these nodes and what each element of their messages means. Synchronization and Gait Events The synchronizer publishes /sync_trigger at 5 Hz until all hubs listed in sensors.yaml deliver messages whose stamps agree within slop_time . It then publishes the concatenated readings on /synced_sensors and resumes triggering if no matched set arrives for 1 s. Because sensors.yaml names every channel, algorithms request signals by name rather than by index. Each detector node passes new samples to a plug-in's detect() and,"}, {"t": "PASE: configure, launch and visualize", "p": "How ExoStack works", "u": "overview.html#pase-configure-launch-and-visualize", "x": "PASE is a PyQt5 application for using the stack without editing files. Its Configuration tab edits the four YAML files, with algorithm lists filled from the plug-in folders. The Launch tab starts the micro-ROS agents and the six stages in order and shows the live ROS 2 graph, and the Visualization tab plots any topic field, with channels selected by name and gait events drawn as markers."}, {"t": "Next steps", "p": "How ExoStack works", "u": "overview.html#next-steps", "x": "Install ExoStack Clone the repository and install ESA, ExoCore, the workspace and PASE. Open → Configure a device Declare the hubs, name the channels, and set up detectors, planners and profiles. Open →"}, {"t": "Installation", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "getting-started.html", "x": "ExoStack has four parts. Each is installed in a different place; the column header of the figure gives the destination, and the highlighted boxes mark where you add your own drivers and algorithms. Repository layout of ExoStack v1.0 (Fig. 6 of the article). Highlighted entries are extension points; the § mark gives the section of this documentation that explains them. Part Runs on Language Installed to Role ESA ESP32 boards on the body C++ (Arduino) ~/Arduino/libraries/ESA reads sensors and drives actuators; publishes/subscribes through micro-ROS ExoCore ( exo_stack ) host PC / Jetson Python, ROS 2 ~/ros2_ws/src/ExoStack/exo_stack (cloned) synchronizes hubs, detects gait events, builds and plays assistance profiles, computes cost, runs HIL exo_stack_resources host YAML, JSON, Python ~/exo_stack_resources everything you edit: configuration, plug-ins, lookup tables; read by ExoCore at start-up PASE host Python, PyQt5 anywhere (run in place) edits the configuration, starts/stops every stage, plots any topic FIXED LOCATION ExoCore and PASE look for exo_stack_resources directly in your home directory . It must be ~/exo_stack_resources , not a sub-folder and not inside the ROS workspace. Four installations, in this order: the ESA firmware environment (Arduino), the micro-ROS agent (host), ExoCore (host) and PASE (host). Allow about 30 minutes."}, {"t": "Requirements", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#requirements", "x": "Item Version Notes Host OS Ubuntu 22.04 on a PC or NVIDIA Jetson ROS 2 Humble (desktop) ros-humble-desktop provides message_filters and sensor_msgs Python 3.10 system Python of Ubuntu 22.04 Arduino IDE 2.x ESP32 board package 2.0.17 (tested) Espressif esp32 core; must match the micro-ROS build below micro_ros_arduino 2.0.7-humble (tested) pre-built micro-ROS client for Arduino Microcontroller ESP32 (ESP-WROOM-32 tested) two I²C buses, ADC, DAC"}, {"t": "Get the code", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#get-the-code", "x": "One git clone fetches all four parts; each is then installed from its own folder. Cloning into the ROS 2 workspace lets colcon find ExoCore in place; the folders without a ROS package carry a COLCON_IGNORE file. terminal $ mkdir -p ~/ros2_ws/src && cd ~/ros2_ws/src $ git clone https://github.com/debadrata/ExoStack.git $ cd ExoStack # paths below are relative to this folder"}, {"t": "A. ESA firmware environment", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#a-esa-firmware-environment", "x": "Install the Arduino IDE . In Boards Manager , install esp32 by Espressif Systems at the version listed above. Download the Humble release of micro_ros_arduino (GitHub: micro-ROS/micro_ros_arduino ) as a ZIP and add it with Sketch → Include Library → Add .ZIP Library . Copy every folder inside ESA/PREREQUISITE_LIBRARIES_COPY_TO_ARDUINO_LIBRARIES/ into ~/Arduino/libraries/ . Copy the library folder ESA/ESA/ into ~/Arduino/libraries/ so that you obtain ~/Arduino/libraries/ESA/src/ESA.h . Restart the IDE. Open File → Examples → ESA → Publisher → hub_right , select your ESP32 board and press Verify . A clean compile confirms the installation. Library (copied in step 3) Version Used by AS5600 0.6.7 magnetic joint encoder driver Adafruit_BusIO 1.17.4 dependency of the PWM driver Adafruit_PWM_Servo_Driver_Library 3.0.3 PCA9685 servo driver SparkFun_BNO080_Cortex_Based_IMU 1.1.12 BNO085 IMU driver SparkFun_Qwiic_Scale_NAU7802_Arduino_Library 1.0.6 NAU7802 load-cell amplifier driver light_CD74HC4067 1.0.1 analog multiplexer (reserved for future use) ONE COPY OF ESA ONLY Keep exactly one copy of the ESA library in ~/Arduino/libraries . Remove any older sensor-suite library from earlier releases; two libraries defining the same classes cause duplicate-symbol errors."}, {"t": "B. micro-ROS agent (host)", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#b-micro-ros-agent-host", "x": "The agent bridges the ESP32 boards to the ROS 2 graph. PASE starts it for you, but it must be built once. terminal $ source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash $ mkdir -p ~/microros_ws/src && cd ~/microros_ws $ git clone -b humble https://github.com/micro-ROS/micro_ros_setup.git src/micro_ros_setup $ sudo apt update && rosdep update && rosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -y $ colcon build && source install/local_setup.bash $ ros2 run micro_ros_setup create_agent_ws.sh $ ros2 run micro_ros_setup build_agent.sh"}, {"t": "C. ExoCore", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#c-exocore", "x": "terminal $ cd ~/ros2_ws/src/ExoStack $ pip3 install -r exo_stack/requirements.txt # numpy, scipy, pyyaml, cmaes, bayesian-optimization==1.4.3 $ cp -r exo_stack_resources ~/ # must end up as ~/exo_stack_resources $ cd ~/ros2_ws && colcon build --packages-select exo_stack WHY THE VERSION PIN OPT_BAYESIAN uses the acquisition-function interface of bayesian-optimization 1.4. Version 2.0 removed it."}, {"t": "D. PASE GUI", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#d-pase-gui", "x": "terminal $ cd ~/ros2_ws/src/ExoStack/PASE_GUI $ pip3 install -r requirements.txt # PyQt5, pyqtgraph, ruamel.yaml, ... $ python3 main.py # run PASE from this folder"}, {"t": "E. Shell environment", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#e-shell-environment", "x": "Every terminal that runs ExoCore or PASE needs all three environments. Append to ~/.bashrc once: ~/.bashrc source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash source ~/microros_ws/install/local_setup.bash source ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash"}, {"t": "F. Verify", "p": "Installation", "u": "getting-started.html#f-verify", "x": "Check Command Expected ExoCore executables ros2 pkg executables exo_stack includes sync , gait_event_detector , mplanner , actuation_profile , cost , hil Launch files ros2 launch exo_stack + Tab five *_launch.py files Resources in place ls ~/exo_stack_resources/config gait_events.yaml mplanner.yaml profile.yaml sensors.yaml Agent ros2 run micro_ros_agent micro_ros_agent --help usage text PASE cd PASE_GUI && python3 main.py window with Configuration, Launch, Visualization tabs Screenshot S2.1-1. PASE main window on the Configuration tab: the Sensors, Gait Events, Motion Planners and Profiles panels, filled from the YAML files."}, {"t": "Configuration", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "configuration.html", "x": "ESA sketch ──► sensors.yaml ──► gait_events.yaml ──► mplanner.yaml ──► profile.yaml ◄── lookup_table/*.json (per board) (hubs, channels) (event detectors) (planners) (profile source, cost, HIL) This value … … must equal this value sketch setupPublisher( topic , …) sensors.yaml → hubs.<hub>.topic order and count of channels the board publishes order and count of names in hubs.<hub>.sensors gait_events.yaml → <event>.output_topic mplanner.yaml → <planner>.gait_event_topic mplanner.yaml planner key profile.yaml → <profile>.planner_name mplanner.yaml → <planner>.output_topic sketch setupActuation( topic , …) on the actuation board algorithm names in any YAML a file of that name (and a class of the same name) in its plug-in folder"}, {"t": "A. ESA sketch: sensing hub", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#a-esa-sketch-sensing-hub", "x": "A board is declared , not programmed: list the devices, then call the set-up functions in the order shown. Highlighted values are the ones you change. hub_right.ino #include \"ESA.h\" SoftExoWalk exo; DeviceConfig deviceList[] = { // type addr/pin name extra_info1 misc1 misc2 { I2C 1 , 0x4A 2 , \"IMU_T\" 3 , MAIN 4 , 9 5 }, // thigh IMU, 9 channels { I2C, 0x4A, \"IMU_S\", AUX1, 9 }, // shank IMU (same address, 2nd bus) { I2C, 0x2A, \"LC_F\", MAIN, -0.0243f, -299.094f }, // load cell: slope, offset { I2C, 0x2A, \"LC_E\", AUX1, -0.0242f, -299.096f }, { AI, 34, \"FSR\", MEDIAN, 3 }, // median filter, window 3 }; const size_t deviceList_len = sizeof(deviceList) / sizeof(deviceList[0]); void setup() { exo.setupCommunication(\"wifi\", \"SSID\", \"PASSWORD\", \"192.168.1.10\", 8888 6 ); // or: setupCommunication(\"usb\") if (exo.initializeSystem()) { exo.setupNode( \"hub_right_node\" 7 ); exo.setupPublisher( \"/hub_right\" 8 , 100 9 ); // topic, rate in Hz exo.bootSystem(); // always last } } void loop() { exo.readSensorsData(); exo.publish(true); // true: rebuild the link automatically if the agent restarts } 1 device type: AI , DI or I2C 2 GPIO pin (AI, DI) or 7-bit address (I²C) 3 short label for your reference 4 meaning depends on the type: see the table below 5 misc1 / misc2: meaning depends on the type and driver 6 Wi-Fi network, password, IP address of the host running the agent, UDP port 7 ROS 2 node name of this board (unique per board) 8 topic name: must equal hubs.<hub>.topic in sensors.yaml 9 publication rate (Hz); also the sampling rate used to design the analog filters What each field of a de"}, {"t": "B. ESA sketch: actuation board", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#b-esa-sketch-actuation-board", "x": "actuator_board.ino #include \"ESA.h\" SoftExoWalk exo; DeviceConfig deviceList[] = { }; // no sensors on this board const size_t deviceList_len = sizeof(deviceList) / sizeof(deviceList[0]); void setup() { exo.setupCommunication(\"wifi\", \"SSID\", \"PASSWORD\", \"192.168.1.10\", 8888); if (exo.initializeSystem()) { exo.setupNode(\"actuator_node\"); // command topic actuator out x1 x2 exo.setupActuation( \"/right/hip_ppr_cmd\" 1 , ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26 2 , 26 3 , 17 4 , 34 5 ); exo.setupActuation(\"/left/hip_ppr_cmd\", ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26, 25, 5, 39); exo.bootSystem(); } } void loop() { exo.actuate(); // all actuators; exo.actuate(\"/right/hip_ppr_cmd\") drives only that one } 1 command topic: must equal output_topic of a planner in mplanner.yaml 2 identifier of a registered actuator driver (table below) 3 output: GPIO pin or I²C address 4 first driver-specific value (omit if unused) 5 second driver-specific value (omit if unused) Actuator out x1 x2 Command (Float32) ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26 proportional pressure valve analog output GPIO (25, 26) exhaust valve GPIO pressure-feedback ADC GPIO (omit if none) pressure set-point, kPa ACT_SG90_SERVO servo via PCA9685 PCA9685 I²C address (e.g. 0x40) PWM channel 0–15 PWM frequency, Hz (50) angle, 0–180° LED_TEST test output GPIO n/a n/a > 0 → on, otherwise off Table S2. Driver registries. Each driver enters itself in a registry through one registration line in its header, before setup() runs. ESA then finds the driver for every device without any change to the library. I²C sensor registry Actuator registry Registration line in the driver header REGISTER_I2C_S"}, {"t": "C. sensors.yaml: hubs and channel names", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#c-sensors-yaml-hubs-and-channel-names", "x": "One entry per sensing board. List one name per published channel, in the exact order the board publishes them : devices in deviceList order, each expanded to its channel count. These names are how every algorithm refers to a signal. ~/exo_stack_resources/config/sensors.yaml hubs: hubr 1 : topic: \"/hub_right\" 2 sensors: [ \"thigh_roll\", \"thigh_pitch\", \"thigh_yaw\", \"thigh_omega_x\", \"thigh_omega_y\", \"thigh_omega_z\", 3 \"thigh_acc_x\", \"thigh_acc_y\", \"thigh_acc_z\", \"shank_roll\", \"shank_pitch\", \"shank_yaw\", \"shank_omega_x\", \"shank_omega_y\", \"shank_omega_z\", \"shank_acc_x\", \"shank_acc_y\", \"shank_acc_z\", \"force_flexion\", \"force_extension\", \"fsr\"] hubl: topic: \"/hub_left\" sensors: [ ... same 21 names ... ] slop_time: 0.010 4 output_topic: \"/synced_sensors\" 1 hub key: used as [hub, name] in the other files 2 the board's publisher topic 3 channel names in publication order 4 seconds; messages from different hubs closer than this are combined into one sample Device row (sketch) Channels Slots Names in sensors.yaml IMU_T (BNO085, misc1 = 9) 9 0–8 thigh_roll … thigh_acc_z IMU_S (BNO085, misc1 = 9) 9 9–17 shank_roll … shank_acc_z LC_F (NAU7802) 1 18 force_flexion LC_E (NAU7802) 1 19 force_extension FSR (AI) 1 20 fsr CHECK THE CHANNEL COUNT With the board running, ros2 topic echo /hub_right --once shows range_max = number of channels and range_min = number of devices. The sensors list must have exactly range_max names. Keep output_topic: \"/synced_sensors\" ; the cost node subscribes to that name."}, {"t": "D. gait_events.yaml: event detectors", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#d-gait-events-yaml-event-detectors", "x": "~/exo_stack_resources/config/gait_events.yaml events: right_heel_strike 1 : input_topic: \"/synced_sensors\" sensors: [ [hubr, fsr 2 ]] algorithm: \"HeelStrike\" 3 parameters: [ 2.0, 0.2 4 ] history_size: 5 5 output_topic: \"/heel_strike_right\" 6 1 event key: becomes the node name; one detector node runs per key 2 [hub, channel] pairs passed to the detector, in this order 3 detector plug-in: file and class name in algo/gait_events/ 4 list handed to the detector; meaning per algorithm (table) 5 sliding-window length in samples (PASE: Window Size ) 6 topic on which an Int32 pulse (value 1) is published at each event Detector parameters Fires when HeelStrike [threshold, fraction] the signal rises above threshold and stays there for fraction of the window, after a low window ZeroCrossingPosToNeg [fraction] the signal crosses from positive to negative and stays negative for fraction of the window ZeroCrossingNegToPos [fraction] the reverse crossing Fig. S1. ExoCore elements (a) Gait-event detection with a confidence window HeelStrike · threshold 2.0 V · window N = 10 samples · fraction F = 0.3 → event after k = ⌈ F·N ⌉ = 3 consecutive samples above the threshold 0 1 2 3 FSR (V) 1 2 3 4 5 threshold event event 0 20 40 60 80 100 sample (100 Hz) detector armed disarmed armed disarmed 1 Spike : 2 samples above the threshold, fewer than k : rejected; the detector stays armed 2 Heel strike : k -th consecutive sample above while armed: event published; the detector disarms 3 Chatter : the signal re-crosses the threshold while disarmed: no event 4 Re-arm : a full window below the threshold, "}, {"t": "E. mplanner.yaml: motion planners", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#e-mplanner-yaml-motion-planners", "x": "~/exo_stack_resources/config/mplanner.yaml planners: right_hip_extension 1 : gait_event_topic: \"/right_hip_extension_start\" 2 planner_algorithm: \"LinearInterpolatedList\" 3 profile_type: \"List\" 4 profile_topic: \"/profile/right_hip_extension\" 5 output_topic: \"/right/hip_ppr_cmd\" 6 approach: \"time_based\" 7 rate: 50 8 default_output: 20.0 9 1 planner key: node name; referenced by planner_name in profile.yaml 2 event that (re)starts the profile: an output_topic from gait_events.yaml 3 planner plug-in in planner/ ; its name ends in List or Function ; interpolation compared in Fig. S1c 4 List or Function : must match the suffix of the planner and of the builder 5 any unique name; the profile node publishes the profile here 6 Float32 command to the actuator: same topic as setupActuation 7 time_based : t = milliseconds since the event · gait_phase_based : t = latest value on gait_phase_topic (add that key) 8 command publication rate, Hz 9 command when idle, before the profile starts, after it ends, or on emergency stop Topic (created per planner) Type Use /<planner>/manual_mode Int32 1 = manual mode on, 0 = off (profiles are reset) /<planner>/manual_cmd Float32 in manual mode the output is default_output + manual_cmd /<planner>/profile_status Float32MultiArray [profile number, gait-cycle number], at every gait event"}, {"t": "F. profile.yaml: where the assistance profile comes from", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#f-profile-yaml-where-the-assistance-profile-comes-from", "x": "Each profile feeds one planner. In Deployment mode the parameters come from a lookup table indexed by walking speed (and optionally intent); in HIL mode they come from an optimizer that is scored by a cost function. Key Mode Meaning planner_name both planner key in mplanner.yaml mode both Deployment or HIL profile_builder_algo both builder in algo/builder/ (table below); suffix must match the planner speed_required , speed_topic Deployment use walking speed (Float32 topic) to index the table intent_required , intent_topic Deployment also index by intent (Float32); intent 0 triggers an emergency stop lookup_algo , lookup_table Deployment plug-in in algo/lookup_algo/ ; JSON file in lookup_table/ (name without .json) rate Deployment profile republication rate, Hz (default 50) hil_algo HIL optimizer in algo/hil_optimizer/ (upper-case name) hil_output_topic HIL topic on which candidates are sent to the profile node (any unique name) hil_hyperparams HIL optimizer settings (next table) cost_algo , cost_params HIL cost plug-in in algo/cost/ ; list passed to its constructor cost_topic HIL topic on which [profile number, cost] is returned (any unique name) sensors HIL [hub, channel] pairs given to the cost function, in this order gait_cycles_to_skip , _to_consider HIL cycles discarded after each new candidate, then cycles averaged into one cost external_sensors_topic HIL, optional Float32MultiArray stream (e.g. metabolic, EMG) used instead of internal sensors additional_gait_marker_topic HIL, optional Int32 marker appended as an extra column for the cost function Optimizer hil_hyperp"}, {"t": "G. Lookup tables", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#g-lookup-tables", "x": "lookup_table/speed_list_lookuptable_2.json (speed only) { \"3.5\": [50.0, 200.0, 500.0, 600.0, 500.0, 800.0], ← speed -> builder inputs \"4.0\": [200.0, 300.0, 100.0, 500.0, 150.0, 800.0] } lookup_table/speed_intent_list_lookuptable_1.json (intent and speed) { \"0.0\": [500.0, 600.0, 100.0, 800.0], ← intent -> builder inputs ... \"1.0\": { \"3.5\": [500.0, 600.0, 100.0, 800.0], ← ... or intent -> speed -> inputs \"4.0\": [400.0, 550.0, 150.0, 800.0] } } Fig. S1b follows one row of such a table to the encoded profile. Lookup_Speed_Segmentwise uses the nearest speed key (clamped at the ends); Lookup_Speed_Interpolated interpolates between keys; Lookup_Speed_Intent_Segmentwise reads the two-level table. Write keys exactly as floats ( \"1.0\" , not \"1\" ): incoming values are \" \"matched as text."}, {"t": "H. Configuring in PASE (Configuration tab)", "p": "Configuration", "u": "configuration.html#h-configuring-in-pase-configuration-tab", "x": "The four panels edit the four YAML files, left to right. Lists of hubs, channels, events, planners and algorithms are filled from the saved files and from the plug-in folders, so you select rather than type. Sensors : + Add Hub ; enter Hub Name , Topic Name and the channel names in Sensors (comma-separated, publication order); set Slop time and Output topic ; press Save YAML . Gait Events : + Add Event ; set Event Name , Input Topic Name ; choose hub and channel under Select Sensors ( + Add Sensor for more); pick the algorithm; fill Parameters and Window Size ; set Output Topic Name ; Save YAML . Motion Planner : + Add Planner ; choose the Gait Event Topic and Planner Algorithm from the lists; set Profile Type , Profile Topic , Output Topic , Approach ( time_based ; phase-based planners are set in the YAML file), Rate , Default Output ; Save YAML . Profile : + Add Profile ; choose Planner Name and Mode . For Deployment set the builder, Speed/Intent Required , Lookup Table , Lookup Algorithm , Rate . For HIL choose the optimizer, add one + Add Dimension row ( Mean / Lower Bound / Upper Bound ) per builder input, then under Define Cost Function set the cost algorithm, Cost Params , Cost Topic , Sensors for Cost and the gait cycles to skip and consider; Save YAML . SAVE FROM LEFT TO RIGHT Saving a panel refreshes the lists of the panels to its right. A plug-in file you add to a folder appears in the corresponding list the next time PASE starts. Screenshot S2.2-1. Sensors and Gait Events panels: the two hubs with their channel names, and the heel-strike detectors, each reading "}, {"t": "Running the stack", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "running.html", "x": "# Stage PASE (Launch tab) Terminal command Check that it works 1 Hubs (micro-ROS agent) HUB CONNECTIONS → Connect Hubs Wi-Fi: ros2 run micro_ros_agent micro_ros_agent udp4 --port 8888 USB: … serial --dev /dev/ttyUSB0 -b 115200 power the boards; ros2 topic list shows /hub_right 2 Synchronizer SYNCHRONIZER → Start Sync ros2 run exo_stack sync ros2 topic hz /synced_sensors 3 Gait detection GAIT DETECTION → Start Detection ros2 launch exo_stack gait_event_detector_launch.py ros2 topic echo /heel_strike_right while walking 4 Motion planning MOTION PLANNING → Start Planning ros2 launch exo_stack mplanner_launch.py output topics carry default_output 5 Profiles PROFILE MANAGER → Start Profile ros2 launch exo_stack profile_launch.py ros2 topic echo /profile/right_hip_extension 6 Cost (HIL) COST COMPUTATION → Start Computation ros2 launch exo_stack cost_launch.py cost logged after the considered cycles 7 HIL optimizer HIL OPTIMIZER → Start Optimization ros2 launch exo_stack hil_launch.py candidate #1 logged START THE OPTIMIZER LAST The HIL optimizer sends its first candidate as soon as it starts. Start it only after the profile, planner and cost stages are running, otherwise the first candidate is lost and the optimizer waits indefinitely for its cost. In Deployment mode, stop at stage 5. Each launch file starts one node per entry of its YAML file, named <key>_event , <key>_planner , <key>_profile , <key>_cost , <key>_hil . To run a single entry while debugging, pass its key: ros2 run exo_stack gait_event_detector right_heel_strike (likewise mplanner , actuation_profile , cost , hil "}, {"t": "PASE GUI", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "gui.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "Three tabs", "p": "PASE GUI", "u": "gui.html#three-tabs", "x": "PASE is a PyQt5 application for using the stack without editing files. Its Configuration tab edits the four YAML files, with algorithm lists filled from the plug-in folders. The Launch tab starts the micro-ROS agents and the six stages in order and shows the live ROS 2 graph, and the Visualization tab plots any topic field, with channels selected by name and gait events drawn as markers. Configuration tab Four linked YAML editors; lists come from the saved files and plug-in folders. Open → Launch tab Start and stop the agents and the six stages; see which nodes are alive. Open →"}, {"t": "Visualization tab", "p": "PASE GUI", "u": "gui.html#visualization-tab", "x": "The Visualization tab plots any field of any topic live, with a rolling 10-second window. The topic tree is on the left, the plot area on the right. To … Do this update the topic list press 🔄 Refresh Topics (after starting new stages) plot a signal expand a topic in the tree and drag a field onto a plot (“Drop a topic field here”). Several fields can share one plot plot a sensor by name expand /synced_sensors : it is listed as hub → channel using sensors.yaml , so you never count array indices add a plot right-click an empty part of the plot area → Add Blank Plot split the screen right-click a plot → Split Vertically or Split Horizontally ; repeat for a grid remove a plot right-click the plot → Remove Plot show events as markers right-click the plot → choose the curve → Event : every message is drawn as a dashed vertical line. Use it for gait-event topics such as /heel_strike_right return to a line plot right-click → curve → Continuous change a curve colour right-click the curve's legend label → choose a colour freeze the display ▶/⏸ pauses and resumes all plots (data keeps arriving) save the arrangement 💾 Save Layout → JSON file with plots, splits, curves, modes and colours restore it 📂 Load Layout → the same JSON file A USEFUL TUNING LAYOUT Split into three rows: (1) /synced_sensors → hubr → fsr with /heel_strike_right in Event mode on the same plot, to check detection timing; (2) /right/hip_ppr_cmd , the command sent to the valve; (3) /synced_sensors → hubr → force_extension , the force actually delivered. Save it once and load it at every session. Screenshot S2.4-1. Vis"}, {"t": "ESA drivers", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "extend-esa.html", "x": "How to read the templates In every template, MYSENS 1 marks a value you change; the number points to its explanation below the template."}, {"t": "I²C sensor driver", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#i2c-sensor-driver", "x": "A new I²C sensor is one header and one source file in src/sensing/I2C/ . The header ends with a registration line; at program start the driver enters itself in the I²C registry (Table S2) under its bus–address pair, and any device-list row with that pair uses it. No other file of the library is edited. Item Convention Example (shipped) Location ESA/src/sensing/I2C/ n/a Files <DEV>.h and <DEV>.cpp NAU7802.h , NAU7802.cpp Functions <DEV>__INIT_ and <DEV>__READ_ : two underscores before, one after NAU7802__INIT_ , NAU7802__READ_ Registration one REGISTER_I2C_SENSOR line per address and bus, in the header REGISTER_I2C_SENSOR(0x2A, MAIN, …) Vendor library installed in ~/Arduino/libraries/ SparkFun_Qwiic_Scale_NAU7802…"}, {"t": "Template: header", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#template-header", "x": "src/sensing/I2C/MYSENS.h #pragma once #include \"I2C_Manager.h\" #include \"MySensorLib.h\" 1 // the chip's Arduino library bool MYSENS 2 __INIT_(I2C_Sens_Ctx* sens); bool MYSENS 2 __READ_(I2C_Sens_Ctx* sens); // address bus init function read function info1, info2 (optional) REGISTER_I2C_SENSOR( 0x48 3 , MAIN 4 , MYSENS__INIT_, MYSENS__READ_, 1.0, 0.0 5 ); REGISTER_I2C_SENSOR(0x48, AUX1, MYSENS__INIT_, MYSENS__READ_, 1.0, 0.0); // same chip on the second bus 1 header of the vendor library for the chip 2 device name <DEV> ; use the same name for the file, both functions and in the .cpp 3 the chip's 7-bit I²C address; add one line per address the chip can take 4 MAIN (SDA 21 / SCL 22) or AUX1 (SDA 19 / SCL 18); add a line per bus you wire it to 5 values of info1 , info2 handed to the driver for this bus and address (e.g. calibration constants)"}, {"t": "Template: source", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#template-source", "x": "src/sensing/I2C/MYSENS.cpp #include \" MYSENS 1 .h\" static constexpr uint8_t N_CH = 2 2 ; // channels this device publishes bool MYSENS__INIT_(I2C_Sens_Ctx* sens) { if (!sens) return false; TwoWire& wire = (sens->line == MAIN) ? Wire : Wire1; // bus chosen in the device list MySensor* dev = new MySensor(); 3 // one driver object per instance for (int i = 0; i <= _MAX_SENSOR_INIT_RETRIES; i++) { if ( dev->begin(sens->addr, wire) 4 ) { dev->setRate(100); 5 // optional chip configuration deviceList[sens->idx].data_per_device = N_CH; // declare the channel count sensorIndices += N_CH; // reserve the slots in the message sens->sens_instance = (void*)dev; // keep the object for READ_ return true; } delay(5); } return false; // device reported as not ready } bool MYSENS__READ_(I2C_Sens_Ctx* sens) { if (!sens || !sens->sens_instance) { sensorIndices += N_CH; return false; } MySensor* dev = (MySensor*)sens->sens_instance; if (! dev->dataReady() 6 ) { // no new sample this cycle: sensorIndices += N_CH; // skip the slots, keep the previous values return false; } msg.intensities.data[sensorIndices++] = dev->readX() 7 * sens->info1 + sens->info2; msg.intensities.data[sensorIndices++] = dev->readY() 7 ; return true; // exactly N_CH values, in a fixed order } 1 include your own header 2 number of values the device publishes every cycle, the length of its slot range 3 the vendor driver class (or your own register-level code) 4 the call that starts the chip at sens->addr on wire ; must return true on success 5 any one-time chip set-up (range, rate, gain); delete if not needed 6 the call that"}, {"t": "Use it", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#use-it", "x": "Copy both files into ~/Arduino/libraries/ESA/src/sensing/I2C/ and install the vendor library. Add a row to the sketch: { I2C, 0x48, \"MYS\", MAIN }, . Add N_CH names to the hub in sensors.yaml , at the position of the new row. Compile and upload; ros2 topic echo <hub topic> --once shows the new range_max . DRIVER RULES Include the header only from its own .cpp ; including it elsewhere registers the driver twice. A bus–address pair belongs to one driver. The registry has a fixed capacity, MAX_I2C_SENS in I2C_Manager.h (8 in v1.0, all taken by the shipped drivers): delete registration lines for addresses you do not wire, or raise the constant. READ_ runs every cycle: no delay() , no blocking waits, no Serial output. Always advance sensorIndices by N_CH , even when you write nothing, so that every later channel stays in its slot."}, {"t": "Actuator driver", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#actuator-driver", "x": "A new actuator is one header and one source file in src/actuation/ . The header registers the driver under an identifier , added once to the actuator list in actuation.h ; the sketch selects the driver by that identifier in setupActuation() . Item Convention Example (shipped) Location ESA/src/actuation/ n/a Files ACT_<DEV>.h and ACT_<DEV>.cpp ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26.h/.cpp Functions <DEV>__INIT_ and <DEV>__ACTUATE_ FESTO_VEAB_L26__INIT_ , FESTO_VEAB_L26__ACTUATE_ Identifier ACT_<DEV> : added to the ActuatorType list in actuation.h , used in setupActuation() ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26"}, {"t": "Template: identifier", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#template-identifier", "x": "src/actuation/actuation.h (excerpt) // UPDATE BELOW ENUM FOR NEW ACTUATOR typedef enum { NO_ACT = 0, ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26, ACT_SG90_SERVO, LED_TEST, ACT_MYACT 1 , // new identifier, always before ACTUATOR_COUNT ACTUATOR_COUNT } ActuatorType; 1 identifier of the new actuator; the same word is used in its registration line and in the sketch"}, {"t": "Template: header", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#template-header-1", "x": "src/actuation/ACT_MYACT.h #pragma once #include \"actuation.h\" void MYACT 1 __INIT_(ActInfo* sub); void MYACT 1 __ACTUATE_(ActInfo* sub); REGISTER_ACTUATOR( ACT_MYACT 2 , MYACT__INIT_, MYACT__ACTUATE_) 1 driver name <DEV> , used for both functions 2 the identifier added to actuation.h ; use it in setupActuation()"}, {"t": "Template: source", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#template-source-1", "x": "src/actuation/ACT_MYACT.cpp #include \" ACT_MYACT 1 .h\" void MYACT__INIT_(ActInfo* sub) { // once, during bootSystem() if (!sub) return; pinMode(sub->out_gpio_i2caddr, OUTPUT); 2 // 'out' argument of setupActuation() if (sub->extra_info1 > 0) pinMode(sub->extra_info1, OUTPUT); 3 // 'x1', optional } void MYACT__ACTUATE_(ActInfo* sub) { // every loop, from actuate() if (!sub) return; float cmd = sub->val; // latest command (0 before the first message) int duty = constrain((int)(cmd * 2.55f), 0, 255); 4 // convert the physical set-point analogWrite(sub->out_gpio_i2caddr, duty); 5 // drive the hardware } 1 include your own header 2 one-time set-up of the output (pin mode, I²C driver object, PWM frequency …) 3 use extra_info1 / extra_info2 (the x1, x2 arguments) for any extra pins or settings; omitted arguments arrive as 0 4 the conversion from the commanded physical quantity (kPa, degrees, %) to the hardware unit 5 the hardware call ( analogWrite , digitalWrite , an I²C driver call …) ActInfo field Type Comes from val float latest Float32 on the command topic out_gpio_i2caddr int8 out argument of setupActuation() extra_info1 , extra_info2 int8 x1 , x2 arguments (−128…127; 0 if omitted) topicName const char* command topic Optional: smooth an analog feedback signal A driver can filter a feedback input with the library's software filters, as the pneumatic valve driver does with its pressure sensor. Register the pin in INIT_ , read it in ACTUATE_ : feedback filter // in MYACT__INIT_ : act_fb_filter_config[_act_fb_idx].filter_type = 1 1 ; // 1 = moving average (filter number = extra_"}, {"t": "Use it", "p": "ESA drivers", "u": "extend-esa.html#use-it-1", "x": "Add ACT_MYACT, to the ActuatorType list in ~/Arduino/libraries/ESA/src/actuation/actuation.h , before ACTUATOR_COUNT . Copy both files into ~/Arduino/libraries/ESA/src/actuation/ . In the actuation sketch: exo.setupActuation(\"/my/cmd\", ACT_MYACT, out, x1, x2); Set the same topic as output_topic of a planner in mplanner.yaml . Compile and upload; test with ros2 topic pub --once /my/cmd std_msgs/msg/Float32 \"{data: 50.0}\" . DRIVER RULES Include the header only from its own .cpp . ACTUATE_ runs every loop for every actuator: keep it short and non-blocking. Variables declared at file scope are shared by all instances of the driver. If a board may run several, keep per-instance state in a small table keyed by sub->out_gpio_i2caddr . Choose the zero command to be a safe state of the hardware: the driver receives 0 until the first message arrives."}, {"t": "ExoCore plug-ins", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "extend-exocore.html", "x": "How to read the templates In every template, MYSENS 1 marks a value you change; the number points to its explanation below the template. Plug-in Folder (in ~/exo_stack_resources) Class name Constructor Method called Named in Gait-event detector algo/gait_events/ = file name (window_size, parameters) detect(values) → 1 | 0 gait_events.yaml → algorithm Profile builder algo/builder/ = file name, ends in _List or _Function () buildprofile(values) → list profile.yaml → profile_builder_algo Lookup algorithm algo/lookup_algo/ = file name (json_path) readlookuptable(args) → list profile.yaml → lookup_algo HIL optimizer algo/hil_optimizer/ = file name, UPPER CASE (**hil_hyperparams) get_next_candidate() , submit_result() , get_best_so_far() , is_done() profile.yaml → hil_algo Cost function algo/cost/ = file name (*cost_params) compute(data) → float profile.yaml → cost_algo Planner planner/ = file name, ends in List or Function () next_point(t) → float ; reads self.profile mplanner.yaml → planner_algorithm THE THREE NAMING RULES The class name is identical to the file name without .py . Builders end in _List or _Function , planners in List or Function ; a builder and the planner it feeds must end in the same word (and match profile_type ). Optimizer files and classes are written in upper case, the node converts the configured name to upper case."}, {"t": "A. Gait-event detector", "p": "ExoCore plug-ins", "u": "extend-exocore.html#a-gait-event-detector", "x": "algo/gait_events/RisingEdge.py class RisingEdge 1 : def __init__(self, window_size=10, parameters=None): self.window_size = window_size # history_size in gait_events.yaml p = parameters or [] # parameters list in gait_events.yaml self. threshold 2 = p[0] if len(p) > 0 else 0.5 3 self.window = [] def detect(self, values): # values: one float per [hub, channel] listed under 'sensors', same order self.window.append(values[0]) if len(self.window) > self.window_size: self.window.pop(0) if len(self.window) < 2: return 0 fired = self.window[-2] <= self.threshold < self.window[-1 4 ] return 1 if fired else 0 # 1 = publish one event pulse 1 class name = file name; write it under algorithm 2 your parameter names … 3 … and their defaults, read from parameters in order 4 the detection rule, evaluated once per synchronized sample"}, {"t": "B. Profile builder", "p": "ExoCore plug-ins", "u": "extend-exocore.html#b-profile-builder", "x": "algo/builder/Ramp_List.py class Ramp 1 _List: def __init__(self): self.default_value = 20.0 # level at the start and end of the profile def buildprofile(self, values): # values: list from the lookup table (Deployment) or the optimizer (HIL) if len(values) != 4 2 : raise ValueError(\"expected [start, rise_end, peak, end]\") start, rise_end, peak, end = values 3 points = [(start, self.default_value), (rise_end, peak), (end, self.default_value) 4 ] profile = [1.0, start] # 1.0 = List profile, then the start time for t, v in points: profile += [t, v] # waypoints, times strictly increasing profile.append(end) # end time return profile 1 name ending in _List (waypoints) or _Function (coefficients) 2 number of input values: the optimizer dimension or the length of each lookup-table row 3 the meaning of each input value 4 the waypoints (time or gait phase, value). A _Function builder returns [2.0, start, c0, c1, …, cn, end] instead"}, {"t": "C. Lookup algorithm", "p": "ExoCore plug-ins", "u": "extend-exocore.html#c-lookup-algorithm", "x": "algo/lookup_algo/Lookup_Speed_Nearest.py import json class Lookup_Speed_Nearest 1 : def __init__(self, json_path): # file named by lookup_table in profile.yaml with open(json_path) as f: self.table = json.load(f) self.keys = sorted(float(k) for k in self.table) def readlookuptable(self, args): # args: [speed], or [speed, intent] when intent_required is true x = float(args[0]) key = min(self.keys, key=lambda k: abs(k - x)) 2 return self.table[str(key)] # list of builder input values 1 class name = file name 2 the selection rule: nearest key here; interpolate between keys, or index by intent first"}, {"t": "D. HIL optimizer", "p": "ExoCore plug-ins", "u": "extend-exocore.html#d-hil-optimizer", "x": "algo/hil_optimizer/OPT_RANDOM.py import random class OPT_RANDOM 1 : def __init__(self, bounds=None, max_iters=20, maximize=True 2 ): # = keys of hil_hyperparams self.bounds, self.max_iters, self.maximize = bounds, max_iters, maximize self.n = 0 self.best_point, self.best_score = None, None def get_next_candidate(self): # one value per builder input return [lo + (hi - lo) * random.random() for lo, hi in self.bounds 3 ] def submit_result(self, candidate, cost): # averaged cost of the candidate just evaluated self.n += 1 if self.best_score is None or (cost > self.best_score) == self.maximize: self.best_point, self.best_score = candidate, cost pass 4 # update your model here def get_best_so_far(self): return self.best_point, self.best_score def is_done(self): # True: results are saved and the node stops return self.n >= self.max_iters 1 upper-case name = file name 2 constructor arguments; each key under hil_hyperparams must appear here 3 how the next candidate is proposed 4 how a new (candidate, cost) pair updates your optimizer The node waits for the cost of each candidate before asking for the next one, and discards any cost carrying a different candidate number, so submit_result always receives the cost of the candidate it proposed."}, {"t": "E. Cost function", "p": "ExoCore plug-ins", "u": "extend-exocore.html#e-cost-function", "x": "algo/cost/PeakForceTracking.py import numpy as np class PeakForceTracking 1 : def __init__(self, target_force 2 ): # cost_params in profile.yaml, in order self.target = target_force def compute(self, data): # data: NumPy array, one row per sample of ONE gait cycle, # one column per [hub, channel] under 'sensors' in profile.yaml force = data[:, 2 3 ] return -abs(float(np.max(force)) - self.target) 4 1 class name = file name 2 constructor arguments, filled positionally from cost_params 3 column of the signal you need (order of sensors ; a gait-marker column is appended last if configured) 4 the per-cycle cost; the node averages it over gait_cycles_to_consider cycles"}, {"t": "F. Planner", "p": "ExoCore plug-ins", "u": "extend-exocore.html#f-planner", "x": "planner/NearestList.py class Nearest 1 List: def __init__(self): self.profile = None # set by the planner node before every call: # List planners: [[t1, v1], [t2, v2], ...] # Function planners: [c0, c1, ..., cn] def next_point(self, t): # t: ms since the gait event (time_based) or gait phase (gait_phase_based) pts = self.profile if t <= pts[0][0]: return pts[0][1] if t >= pts[-1][0]: return pts[-1][1] for (t0, v0), (t1, v1) in zip(pts, pts[1:]): if t0 <= t <= t1: return v0 if (t - t0) < (t1 - t) else v1 2 1 name ending in List or Function 2 the interpolation rule: nearest waypoint here; linear, PCHIP, or a polynomial for Function planners TRY A PLUG-IN WITHOUT THE ROBOT Every plug-in is plain Python and can be imported and called in an interpreter before it is used in the stack, e.g. from Ramp_List import Ramp_List; Ramp_List().buildprofile([0, 150, 60, 400]) . After saving the file, restart the stage that uses it (restart icon in the Launch tab)."}, {"t": "API & reference", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "reference.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "ESA API", "p": "API & reference", "u": "reference.html#esa-api", "x": "Public interface of the SoftExoWalk class in ESA.h . Function Arguments Purpose setupCommunication (\"wifi\", ssid, pass, agent_ip, port) (\"usb\") Selects the micro-ROS transport: Wi-Fi (UDP) or USB (serial). initializeSystem () → bool Configures every deviceList[] row: analog pins, digital input modes, I²C drivers; sizes the message. setupNode (name = \"microROS_Node\") Names the board's ROS 2 node. setupPublisher (topic = \"\", pub_rate = 0) Sensing hub: topic and publication rate in Hz (also the filter design rate). setupActuation (topic, ActuatorType, out, x1 = -1, x2 = -1) Actuation: one command topic per actuator, e.g. ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26 ; up to 5 per board. bootSystem () Creates the publisher, timer, subscriptions and executor; initializes the actuators. Always last. readSensorsData () Reads every device once into the message (call in loop() ). publish (reconnect = false) Spins the executor; with true , rebuilds the link after an agent restart. actuate (topic = \"\") Spins the executor and drives all actuators, or only the one on topic . emergencyShutdown () Stops the board's ROS entities. get_actCount / get_actCMD(id) / get_pubRate / get_debugFlag () Number of actuators, latest command of actuator id , publication rate, debug flag."}, {"t": "ExoCore executables", "p": "API & reference", "u": "reference.html#exocore-executables", "x": "The six executables of the exo_stack package. Launch files start one process per YAML entry. Executable Configured by Started by Role sync n/a ros2 run exo_stack sync synchronizes the hubs into /synced_sensors gait_event_detector gait_events.yaml gait_event_detector_launch.py one detector per entry mplanner mplanner.yaml mplanner_launch.py one motion planner per entry actuation_profile profile.yaml profile_launch.py one profile node per entry (lookup table or HIL candidate) cost profile.yaml (HIL) cost_launch.py per-gait-cycle cost hil profile.yaml (HIL) hil_launch.py HIL optimizer (start it last)"}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "p": "API & reference", "u": "reference.html#troubleshooting", "x": "Symptom Likely cause Remedy Status LED on the board blinks rapidly a ROS entity could not be created start the agent; check IP, port and topic names Board restarts every few seconds watchdog: loop() blocked remove delays and blocking calls from the sketch and drivers USB link drops or never connects the sketch writes to Serial remove all Serial calls when using the USB transport Analog channel stuck at 0 or full scale with Wi-Fi pin on ADC2 move it to GPIO 32–39 New I²C device not found duplicate bus–address pair, registry full, or header included elsewhere see the rules in §2.5 Duplicate-definition errors when compiling two copies of the ESA library keep one folder in ~/Arduino/libraries /synced_sensors silent a configured hub is not publishing, or a topic name differs every hub in sensors.yaml must be running Warning Expected N intensities, got M channel list and board disagree align sensors.yaml with the device list ( §2.2-C ) FileNotFoundError … exo_stack_resources folder not in the home directory move it to ~/exo_stack_resources module has no attribute … class name differs from file name rename one of them ( §2.7 ) Planner warns of a profile-type mismatch suffixes of builder, planner and profile_type differ use List everywhere or Function everywhere HIL stops after candidate #1 optimizer started before profile and cost stages; or cost_topic differs start HIL last ( §2.3 ) Profile number mismatch … Ignoring a late cost from the previous candidate normal once after each change PASE cannot start the agent agent workspace not sourced add it to ~/.bashrc ( §2.1-E )"}, {"t": "ROS 2 and micro-ROS interfaces", "p": "API & reference", "u": "reference.html#ros-2-and-micro-ros-interfaces", "x": "Table S3 lists the topic of every ESA hub and ExoCore node and what each element of its message means. Each node and every message layout is detailed, with examples, under ROS 2 interfaces . Table S3. ROS 2 and micro-ROS interfaces. Every topic that connects the ESA hubs and the ExoCore nodes, grouped by the node that publishes it. Names in angle brackets are set in the file or call shown under them; all types are standard ROS 2 messages. Topic Type From → to Content of the message ESA sensing hub: micro-ROS node, one per board (×N) /<hub topic> setupPublisher(); sensors.yaml sensor_msgs/ LaserScan hub → synchronizer intensities[i] : channel i, in device-list order: AI in volts (0–3.3), DI 0 or 1, I²C in the driver's units; range_min : number of devices; range_max : number of channels; header.stamp : hub time since the last /sync_trigger . Other fields unused. Sent at the setupPublisher() rate. /sync_trigger fixed name std_msgs/ Int32 synchronizer → every hub data = 0, sent at 5 Hz in trigger mode; on reception a hub stores millis() as its time offset. ESA actuation hub: micro-ROS node (×M) /<command topic> setupActuation(); output_topic in mplanner.yaml std_msgs/ Float32 planner → actuation hub data : set-point in the driver's unit (kPa for ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26 , degrees for ACT_SG90_SERVO ). Kept as the actuator's latest command; 0 until the first message. Synchronizer: sync (×1) /synced_sensors output_topic in sensors.yaml std_msgs/ Float32MultiArray synchronizer → detectors, cost evaluators data : the channels of every hub, concatenated in sensors.yaml order (hub by hub, "}, {"t": "Known limitations of v1.0", "p": "API & reference", "u": "reference.html#known-limitations-of-v1-0", "x": "KNOWN LIMITATIONS OF v1.0 Device-list values dev_misc_data and dev_misc_data2 reach the I²C drivers that read them (the BNO085 channel count); the NAU7802 calibration and the AS5600 direction pin come from the drivers' registration lines. Function profiles ( Builder1_Function with PolynomialFunction or CubicPolynomialFunction ) and PCHIPInterpolatedList do not run in v1.0; use a _List builder with LinearInterpolatedList . PASE writes the approach gait_phase , which the planner does not recognise, and adds init_points and gp_params to Bayesian profiles, which OPT_BAYESIAN does not accept: configure phase-based planners and Bayesian profiles in the YAML files. The I²C registry holds 8 entries, all used by the shipped drivers ( §2.5 ). Corrections are planned for the next release."}, {"t": "Validated hardware", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "hardware.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "Two exosuits in action", "p": "Validated hardware", "u": "hardware.html#two-exosuits-in-action", "x": "SoftEXOwalk knee-flexion exosuit : Bayesian HIL personalization. Hip flexion–extension exosuit : CMA-ES personalization."}, {"t": "Configurations of the two exosuits", "p": "Validated hardware", "u": "hardware.html#configurations-of-the-two-exosuits", "x": "ExoStack was validated on two pneumatic exosuits built at CSIR-CMERI. Both are actuated by pSTAMs, pneumatic silicone tube artificial muscles, through proportional pressure regulators, and both run the same ESA library and ExoCore nodes: they differ only in their device lists, YAML files and plug-ins. Fig. 5 of the paper shows both in use, and SoftEXOwalk is described in full in its own article. Configurations SoftEXOwalk (knee) Hip exosuit Assistance bilateral knee flexion bilateral hip flexion and extension Actuators one sensorized pSTAM across each knee, on the posterior side four pSTAMs in a backpack, with tendons to the thighs and a speed-enhancement mechanism that makes them fast enough for walking Sensing hubs two, one per leg, over USB. Each reads an insole FSR (analog), two BNO085 IMUs (I²C), a load cell for validation (analog) and a silicone and carbon-black strain sensor on the pSTAM bladder (analog) two on the thighs, over Wi-Fi, each reading an insole FSR (analog), two BNO085 IMUs (I²C) and two load cells through NAU7802 amplifiers (I²C); two in the backpack, over USB, each reading two encoders (I²C), two muscle strain sensors and two regulator pressures (analog), one of them also a BNO085 IMU (I²C) Actuation hubs two, over USB, each driving one proportional pressure regulator and reading back its pressure (analog) two, over USB, each driving two proportional pressure regulators, one for flexion and one for extension, and four solenoid valves that assist the exhaust of the pSTAMs Rates hubs publish at 100 Hz; actuation loop at 100 Hz hubs publish at 100 Hz; act"}, {"t": "Benchmarks and validation", "p": "Validated hardware", "u": "hardware.html#benchmarks-and-validation", "x": "Benchmarking: We benchmarked ExoStack under ROS 2 Humble on a Jetson AGX Orin, a Jetson Orin Nano, and a LattePanda Delta. Each sensing hub carried ten devices, an I²C sensor on each of its two buses and eight analog inputs, and published at a target rate of 100 Hz. With up to eight such hubs on each computer, the observed rate was 99.5 ± 0.018 Hz, a period of 10.05 ms with a jitter of about 1.8 µs, so the hubs keep their rate as their number grows. With two such hubs and target motion-planner rates of 200 to 500 Hz, Fig. 4 shows the loop rate each computer achieved and the end-to-end latency from a sensing-hub input to an actuation-hub output. The Jetson AGX Orin achieved 99.6–99.9 % of every target with mean latencies of 8–21 ms. The Jetson Orin Nano kept at least 98.9 % up to 400 Hz, with 7–16 ms, and 91.3 % at 500 Hz. The LattePanda Delta achieved 97.0–98.9 % up to 400 Hz and 86.6 % at 500 Hz, where its latency rose to about 50 ms. At 200 and 300 Hz, all three kept at least 97 % of the target with mean latencies below 18 ms. Supplementary §3 details the set-up and results. Validation on two exosuits: Two pneumatic exosuits, actuated by pSTAMs through proportional pressure regulators, ran the same ESA library and ExoCore nodes, differing only in configuration and plug-ins (Fig. 5 , Supplementary §4 ). On the bilateral knee-flexion exosuit SoftEXOwalk , each leg had a USB sensing hub (insole FSR, two BNO085 IMUs, a load cell, and a pSTAM strain sensor), an actuation hub driving one regulator, and a heel-strike detector. Bayesian optimization tuned the amplitude and onset "}, {"t": "Test set-up", "p": "Validated hardware", "u": "hardware.html#test-set-up", "x": "Item Set-up Host computers Jetson AGX Orin, Jetson Orin Nano, LattePanda Delta; ROS 2 Humble, ExoStack v1.0 Sensing hub ESP32 running ESA; ten devices per hub: one I²C sensor on MAIN , one on AUX1 , and eight analog inputs Hub publication target rate 100 Hz ( setupPublisher(topic, 100) ) Experiment 1: regularity up to eight such hubs on each host computer, synchronized; observed publication rate Experiment 2: loop rate and latency two such hubs on each host computer; motion-planner target rates of 200, 300, 400 and 500 Hz; achieved planner rate and end-to-end latency from a sensing-hub input to an actuation-hub output"}, {"t": "Publication regularity", "p": "Validated hardware", "u": "hardware.html#publication-regularity", "x": "On each of the three computers, with up to eight hubs publishing at a target of 100 Hz, the observed rate was 99.5 ± 0.018 Hz (mean ± SD). This is a mean period of 10.05 ms whose spread, σ T ≈ σ f /f², corresponds to a jitter of about 1.8 µs : the hubs keep their rate as their number grows."}, {"t": "Results by computer", "p": "Validated hardware", "u": "hardware.html#results-by-computer", "x": "Target rate (Hz) Jetson AGX Orin Jetson Orin Nano LattePanda Delta 200 99.8 % · 7.5 ms 99.9 % · 7.4 ms 97.0 % · 13.6 ms 300 99.6 % · 8.4 ms 98.9 % · 7.8 ms 98.9 % · 17.8 ms 400 99.9 % · 14.0 ms 99.0 % · 15.5 ms 97.4 % · 32.9 ms 500 99.7 % · 20.6 ms 91.3 % · 17.2 ms 86.6 % · 50.0 ms Each cell: share of the target rate achieved by the motion planner · mean end-to-end latency. Shares as printed in the figure; latency means read from the plotted markers (±0.3 ms). The Jetson AGX Orin achieved 99.6–99.9 % of every target with mean latencies of 8–21 ms. The Jetson Orin Nano kept at least 98.9 % up to 400 Hz (7–16 ms) and 91.3 % at 500 Hz. The LattePanda Delta achieved 97.0–98.9 % up to 400 Hz and 86.6 % at 500 Hz, where its latency rose to about 50 ms. At 200 and 300 Hz all three kept at least 97 % of the target with mean latencies below 18 ms, so a planner rate in this range leaves margin on every host tested. Fig. 5 of the paper. Validation. (a) Bilateral knee-flexion exosuit SoftEXOwalk ; (b) Bayesian HIL optimization; (c) actuation profiles and assistive forces with the personalized parameters. (d) Hip flexion–extension exosuit; (e) extension assistive-force profiles tuned by CMA-ES."}, {"t": "Paper & citation", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "research.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "The paper", "p": "Paper & citation", "u": "research.html#the-paper", "x": "ExoStack: A Modular and Reconfigurable Software Framework for Wearable Robotics Using ROS 2 Debadrata Sarkar et al. arXiv preprint , 2026. Read on arXiv User manual (PDF)"}, {"t": "Abstract", "p": "Paper & citation", "u": "research.html#abstract", "x": "Wearable assistive robots, such as exosuits and exoskeletons, need software that reads many body-worn sensors, recognizes the wearer's gait, decides how much assistance to apply, and drives the actuators, often while an optimizer personalizes the assistance. This software is usually rewritten for each new device, which slows research and hinders reproducibility. We present ExoStack, a modular and reconfigurable software framework built on ROS 2 that provides this complete pipeline as reusable nodes exchanging data over topics. Its firmware library, ESA, turns body-worn microcontroller boards into micro-ROS hubs that publish synchronized sensor data and drive actuators. Its ROS 2 nodes, ExoCore, detect gait events, generate and execute assistance profiles, and run human-in-the-loop (HIL) optimization. Its graphical tool, PASE, configures, launches, and visualizes the stack. New sensors, actuators, and algorithms are added from templates without editing the core, and a new device, with more joints, both legs, or different hardware, is configured rather than coded. We benchmark ExoStack on three embedded computers and validate it on two pneumatic exosuits."}, {"t": "Cite ExoStack", "p": "Paper & citation", "u": "research.html#cite", "x": "BibTeX @article{exostack2026, title = {ExoStack: A Modular and Reconfigurable Software Framework for Wearable Robotics Using ROS 2}, author = {Sarkar, Debadrata and others}, journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:[ARXIV_ID]}, year = {2026}, doi = {10.48550/arXiv.[ARXIV_ID]} }"}, {"t": "Publications using ExoStack", "p": "Paper & citation", "u": "research.html#publications-using-exostack", "x": "D. Sarkar, S. Chakraborty, S. Sen, S. S. Roy, and A. Arora, “SoftEXOwalk: A human-in-the-loop optimized soft exosuit for assisting knee flexion using sensorized pneumatic muscles,” IEEE Transactions on Medical Robotics and Bionics , 2026, doi: 10.1109/TMRB.2026.3722336 . Used ExoStack in your work? Tell us through GitHub Issues and we will list it here."}, {"t": "Team & contact", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "about.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "Team", "p": "Team & contact", "u": "about.html#team", "x": "Debadrata Sarkar , Soumen Sen , Aman Arora Human Centred Robotics and Cybernetics Group, CSIR–Central Mechanical Engineering Research Institute (CSIR-CMERI), Durgapur, India"}, {"t": "Contact", "p": "Team & contact", "u": "about.html#contact", "x": "For questions, collaborations and bug reports, write to us or open an issue on GitHub. Debadrata Sarkar Human Centred Robotics and Cybernetics Group, CSIR-CMERI, Durgapur deba00126@gmail.com · LinkedIn · dsarkarrobotics.com Aman Arora Human Centred Robotics and Cybernetics Group, CSIR-CMERI, Durgapur aman.cmeri@nic.in · CSIR-CMERI profile · LinkedIn"}, {"t": "Contributing", "p": "Team & contact", "u": "about.html#contributing", "x": "Bug reports, new drivers and plug-ins are welcome. Open an issue describing the change, then a pull request against main on GitHub . New sensors, actuators and algorithms follow the templates in ESA drivers and ExoCore plug-ins ."}, {"t": "License", "p": "Team & contact", "u": "about.html#license", "x": "The ExoStack source code is released under the PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0 : it may be used, modified and shared for research, teaching and other noncommercial purposes; commercial use needs a separate license from the authors. This documentation, the user manual and the website, is released under CC BY-NC 4.0 . The vendor libraries shipped with ESA keep their own licenses ( THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md in the repository). The fonts of this website are distributed under the SIL Open Font License."}, {"t": "Acknowledgments", "p": "Team & contact", "u": "about.html#acknowledgments", "x": "This work was fully supported by the CSIR-National Laboratory Scheme under the ULIP [MLP002613] and the CSIR Mission Mode Project [NCP010303]. This work involved human subjects. Approval of all ethical and experimental procedures was granted by the 7th sitting of the Institutional Ethical Board of CSIR-CMERI, Durgapur, India, on 27 February 2024."}, {"t": "ROS 2 interfaces", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "ros2-interfaces.html", "x": "ExoStack is a set of ROS 2 nodes that talk over topics. This section lists, node by node, what each subscribes to and publishes, and, under Message layouts , what every element of every message means. Click a message type to open its layout. ExoCore nodes and their topics (Fig. 3 of the paper)."}, {"t": "Nodes", "p": "ROS 2 interfaces", "u": "ros2-interfaces.html#nodes", "x": "Node Name at run time Executable Instances ESA sensing hub setupNode(name) sketch on the board ×N ESA actuation hub setupNode(name) sketch on the board ×M Synchronizer generic_sync_node sync ×1 Gait-event detector <event>_event gait_event_detector ×E Profile node <profile>_profile actuation_profile ×P Motion planner <planner>_planner mplanner ×P Cost evaluator <profile>_cost cost ×C HIL optimizer <profile>_hil hil ×H Placeholders are the keys of the YAML entries; launch files start one node per entry. ×C counts the profiles that define a cost and ×H those in HIL mode; both are at most P."}, {"t": "All topics", "p": "ROS 2 interfaces", "u": "ros2-interfaces.html#all-topics", "x": "Topic Message type From → to /<hub topic> sensor_msgs/LaserScan hub layout sensing hub → synchronizer /sync_trigger std_msgs/Int32 sync trigger layout synchronizer → every hub /synced_sensors std_msgs/Float32MultiArray synchronized channels layout synchronizer → detectors, cost evaluators /<event topic> std_msgs/Int32 gait event layout detector → planner /<speed_topic> , /<intent_topic> std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout estimator → profile node /<gait_phase_topic> std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout estimator → planner /<profile_topic> std_msgs/Float32MultiArray profile layout profile node → planner /<output_topic> std_msgs/Float32 set-point layout planner → actuation hub or ROS 2 driver /<planner>/profile_status std_msgs/Float32MultiArray profile status layout planner → cost evaluator /<planner>/manual_mode , …/manual_cmd std_msgs/Int32 manual override layout PASE or you → planner /<cost_topic> std_msgs/Float32MultiArray cost layout cost evaluator → HIL optimizer /<hil_output_topic> std_msgs/Float32MultiArray HIL candidate layout HIL optimizer → profile node /<external_sensors_topic> std_msgs/Float32MultiArray external sensors layout your sensor node → cost evaluator /<additional_gait_marker_topic> std_msgs/Int32 gait marker layout your node → cost evaluator"}, {"t": "Conventions", "p": "ROS 2 interfaces", "u": "ros2-interfaces.html#conventions", "x": "Names in angle brackets are set in a YAML file or a sketch call; each node page lists where. Only /sync_trigger and /<planner>/… follow fixed patterns. Quality of service: every ExoStack topic is best-effort, which suits periodic sensor and command streams. A node of yours may publish best-effort or reliable; to subscribe to an ExoStack topic, use best-effort. The candidate number k is the first element of the profile, status, cost and HIL messages. It ties a measured cost to the parameters that produced it: the cost evaluator counts only cycles of the candidate in profile_status , and the optimizer accepts only a cost carrying the k it proposed."}, {"t": "Inspecting the graph", "p": "ROS 2 interfaces", "u": "ros2-interfaces.html#inspecting-the-graph", "x": "terminal ros2 node list # e.g. /generic_sync_node, /right_heel_strike_event, /profile1_hil ros2 topic list -t # topics with their types ros2 topic echo /right_knee_flexion/profile_status ros2 topic hz /hub_right # publication rate of a hub ros2 topic info -v /synced_sensors # publishers, subscribers and QoS"}, {"t": "ESA hubs", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-esa.html", "x": "ESA hubs are micro-ROS nodes on ESP32 boards. Their topics are named in the sketch, by the calls shown under each topic."}, {"t": "Sensing hub", "p": "ESA hubs", "u": "if-esa.html#sensing-hub", "x": "Node name setupNode(name) , default microROS_Node Runs on ESP32, through the micro-ROS agent (Wi-Fi UDP or USB serial) Instances ×N, one per board Published topics Topic Description Message type /<hub topic> setupPublisher(topic, rate) every channel of the board, at rate Hz sensor_msgs/LaserScan hub layout Subscribed topics Topic Description Message type /sync_trigger re-zeroes the hub clock used for the stamps std_msgs/Int32 sync trigger layout"}, {"t": "Actuation hub", "p": "ESA hubs", "u": "if-esa.html#actuation-hub", "x": "Node name setupNode(name) Instances ×M boards, up to 5 actuators each Subscribed topics Topic Description Message type /<command topic> setupActuation(topic, type, out, x1, x2) set-point of one actuator; one topic per actuator std_msgs/Float32 set-point layout ONE EXECUTOR PER BOARD publish() and actuate() spin the micro-ROS executor with a 10-ms timeout. It runs the publication timer and the sync and command callbacks; publish(true) also restores the agent link after a restart. A board may both sense and actuate. All hub entities are best-effort."}, {"t": "Synchronizer", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-sync.html", "x": "The synchronizer subscribes to every hub listed in sensors.yaml and matches their messages with an approximate-time policy (queue 5, tolerance slop_time ). It starts in trigger mode , publishing /sync_trigger at 5 Hz so that every hub re-zeroes its clock; at the first matched set it switches to sync mode . If no matched set arrives for 1 s, it resets and triggers again. Repeated matches with identical stamps are dropped. Node name generic_sync_node Executable ros2 run exo_stack sync Instances ×1"}, {"t": "Subscribed topics", "p": "Synchronizer", "u": "if-sync.html#subscribed-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<hub topic> (each hub) the hub messages to be matched sensor_msgs/LaserScan hub layout"}, {"t": "Published topics", "p": "Synchronizer", "u": "if-sync.html#published-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /synced_sensors the channels of all hubs, for each matched set std_msgs/Float32MultiArray synchronized channels layout /sync_trigger clock reference for the hubs, 5 Hz in trigger mode std_msgs/Int32 sync trigger layout"}, {"t": "Configuration", "p": "Synchronizer", "u": "if-sync.html#configuration", "x": "The keys that name or shape the topics above; every key is explained in Configuration . Key Meaning hubs.<hub>.topic topic of the hub hubs.<hub>.sensors channel names, in publication order slop_time matching tolerance, s (default 0.01) output_topic name of the synchronized topic (default /synced_sensors)"}, {"t": "Gait-event detector", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-detector.html", "x": "Each detector passes the configured channels to its plug-in's detect() and publishes 1 when the event is confirmed by its confidence window (Fig. S1a in Configuration ). Node name <event>_event Executable gait_event_detector via gait_event_detector_launch.py Instances ×E, one per entry of gait_events.yaml"}, {"t": "Subscribed topics", "p": "Gait-event detector", "u": "if-detector.html#subscribed-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<input_topic> the synchronized channels (normally /synced_sensors ) std_msgs/Float32MultiArray synchronized channels layout"}, {"t": "Published topics", "p": "Gait-event detector", "u": "if-detector.html#published-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<output_topic> 1 at each detected event std_msgs/Int32 gait event layout"}, {"t": "Configuration", "p": "Gait-event detector", "u": "if-detector.html#configuration", "x": "The keys that name or shape the topics above; every key is explained in Configuration . Key Meaning events.<event>.input_topic topic to read events.<event>.sensors [hub, channel] pairs passed to the plug-in events.<event>.algorithm plug-in in algo/gait_events/ events.<event>.parameters plug-in parameters, e.g. [threshold, fraction] events.<event>.history_size window length N, samples events.<event>.output_topic event topic; a planner's gait_event_topic"}, {"t": "Profile node", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-profile.html", "x": "In Deployment , a timer at rate reads the speed (and intent), looks up a table row and publishes the built profile with k = 0. In HIL , the node builds and publishes each candidate once, keeping its k. The profile is sent on the profile_topic of the planner it serves. Node name <profile>_profile Executable actuation_profile via profile_launch.py Instances ×P, one per entry of profile.yaml"}, {"t": "Subscribed topics", "p": "Profile node", "u": "if-profile.html#subscribed-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<speed_topic> walking speed (Deployment, if speed_required) std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout /<intent_topic> locomotion intent (Deployment, if intent_required) std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout /<hil_output_topic> next candidate (HIL) std_msgs/Float32MultiArray HIL candidate layout"}, {"t": "Published topics", "p": "Profile node", "u": "if-profile.html#published-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<profile_topic> the profile for the planner, every 1/rate (Deployment) or per candidate (HIL) std_msgs/Float32MultiArray profile layout"}, {"t": "Configuration", "p": "Profile node", "u": "if-profile.html#configuration", "x": "The keys that name or shape the topics above; every key is explained in Configuration . Key Meaning profiles.<p>.planner_name planner served; its profile_topic is used mode Deployment or HIL profile_builder_algo builder in algo/builder/ lookup_algo lookup plug-in (Deployment) lookup_table JSON table (Deployment) speed_required subscribe to speed_topic speed_topic speed input intent_required subscribe to intent_topic intent_topic intent input rate publication rate in Deployment, Hz hil_output_topic candidate input (HIL)"}, {"t": "Motion planner", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-planner.html", "x": "At every event on gait_event_topic the planner starts its active profile. A profile that arrives while one is playing waits in a buffer and becomes active only between executions, so the assistance never changes mid-stride. At rate it publishes next_point(t) of its plug-in, with t the time since the event ( time_based ) or the gait phase ( gait_phase_based ), and default_output outside the profile window or on an emergency stop. Node name <planner>_planner Executable mplanner via mplanner_launch.py Instances ×P, one per entry of mplanner.yaml"}, {"t": "Subscribed topics", "p": "Motion planner", "u": "if-planner.html#subscribed-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<gait_event_topic> starts the profile std_msgs/Int32 gait event layout /<profile_topic> profile to play, buffered std_msgs/Float32MultiArray profile layout /<gait_phase_topic> time argument (gait_phase_based only) std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout /<planner>/manual_mode manual override on or off std_msgs/Int32 manual override layout /<planner>/manual_cmd set-point in manual mode std_msgs/Float32 manual override layout"}, {"t": "Published topics", "p": "Motion planner", "u": "if-planner.html#published-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<output_topic> actuator set-point, at rate std_msgs/Float32 set-point layout /<planner>/profile_status candidate and gait-cycle count, at each event std_msgs/Float32MultiArray profile status layout"}, {"t": "Configuration", "p": "Motion planner", "u": "if-planner.html#configuration", "x": "The keys that name or shape the topics above; every key is explained in Configuration . Key Meaning planners.<planner>.gait_event_topic event that starts the profile planner_algorithm plug-in in planner/ profile_topic profile input, published by the profile node profile_type List or Function output_topic command topic of the actuator approach time_based or gait_phase_based rate set-point rate, Hz default_output set-point outside the profile window gait_phase_topic phase input for gait_phase_based"}, {"t": "Cost evaluator", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-cost.html", "x": "The cost evaluator follows the planner's profile_status : for each new k it skips gait_cycles_to_skip cycles while the wearer adapts, evaluates its cost plug-in per cycle on the requested channels, external signals and gait marker, and publishes the mean over gait_cycles_to_consider cycles. Node name <profile>_cost Executable cost via cost_launch.py Instances ×C, one per profile with a cost"}, {"t": "Subscribed topics", "p": "Cost evaluator", "u": "if-cost.html#subscribed-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<planner>/profile_status candidate and cycle count std_msgs/Float32MultiArray profile status layout /synced_sensors channels listed in sensors std_msgs/Float32MultiArray synchronized channels layout /<external_sensors_topic> optional external signals std_msgs/Float32MultiArray external sensors layout /<additional_gait_marker_topic> optional in-cycle marker std_msgs/Int32 gait marker layout"}, {"t": "Published topics", "p": "Cost evaluator", "u": "if-cost.html#published-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<cost_topic> mean cost of a candidate std_msgs/Float32MultiArray cost layout"}, {"t": "Configuration", "p": "Cost evaluator", "u": "if-cost.html#configuration", "x": "The keys that name or shape the topics above; every key is explained in Configuration . Key Meaning profiles.<p>.cost_algo plug-in in algo/cost/ cost_params plug-in parameters sensors [hub, channel] pairs used cost_topic output; the optimizer's input external_sensors_topic optional input additional_gait_marker_topic optional input gait_cycles_to_skip cycles discarded per candidate gait_cycles_to_consider cycles averaged"}, {"t": "HIL optimizer", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-hil.html", "x": "The node asks its plug-in for a candidate ( get_next_candidate() ), publishes it with a new k, and passes each matching cost back ( submit_result() ). Costs with another k are discarded. When the optimizer stops, every round and the best candidate ( get_best_so_far() ) are written to ~/exo_stack_resources/hil_results/HIL_<optimizer>_<cost>_…_<time>.csv . Node name <profile>_hil Executable hil via hil_launch.py (start it last) Instances ×H, one per profile in HIL mode"}, {"t": "Subscribed topics", "p": "HIL optimizer", "u": "if-hil.html#subscribed-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<cost_topic> mean cost of the current candidate std_msgs/Float32MultiArray cost layout"}, {"t": "Published topics", "p": "HIL optimizer", "u": "if-hil.html#published-topics", "x": "Topic Description Message type /<hil_output_topic> next candidate std_msgs/Float32MultiArray HIL candidate layout"}, {"t": "Configuration", "p": "HIL optimizer", "u": "if-hil.html#configuration", "x": "The keys that name or shape the topics above; every key is explained in Configuration . Key Meaning profiles.<p>.hil_algo OPT_CMA_ES, OPT_BAYESIAN or your plug-in hil_hyperparams.mean start point, one value per dimension hil_hyperparams.bounds [min, max] per dimension hil_hyperparams.maximize true = maximize the cost (PASE: Purpose) hil_hyperparams.sigma, max_generations, population_size, n_max_resampling CMA-ES settings hil_output_topic candidate output; the profile node's input cost_topic cost input"}, {"t": "External inputs", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "if-external.html", "x": "ExoStack accepts signals from your own nodes and hands its set-points to any ROS 2 driver. Publish on the topic named in the YAML file, with the type and layout shown, and use best-effort QoS to match ExoStack. Topic Description Message type /<speed_topic> walking speed for the lookup table → profile node std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout /<intent_topic> locomotion intent → profile node std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout /<gait_phase_topic> gait phase → planner std_msgs/Float32 estimator input layout /<external_sensors_topic> metabolic, EMG or other signals → cost evaluator std_msgs/Float32MultiArray external sensors layout /<additional_gait_marker_topic> in-cycle marker → cost evaluator std_msgs/Int32 gait marker layout"}, {"t": "Driving another actuator", "p": "External inputs", "u": "if-external.html#driving-another-actuator", "x": "A planner's output_topic is an ordinary std_msgs/Float32 set-point, so a ROS 2 driver of any actuator can subscribe to it instead of an actuation hub."}, {"t": "A minimal estimator", "p": "External inputs", "u": "if-external.html#a-minimal-estimator", "x": "speed_estimator.py import rclpy from rclpy.node import Node from rclpy.qos import QoSProfile, QoSReliabilityPolicy from std_msgs.msg import Float32 class SpeedEstimator(Node): def __init__(self): super().__init__(\"speed_estimator\") qos = QoSProfile(depth=10, reliability=QoSReliabilityPolicy.BEST_EFFORT) self.pub = self.create_publisher(Float32, \"/walking_speed\", qos) # speed_topic in profile.yaml self.create_timer(0.1, self.tick) # 10 Hz def tick(self): self.pub.publish(Float32(data=3.5)) # your estimate here rclpy.init() rclpy.spin(SpeedEstimator())"}, {"t": "Message layouts", "p": "ExoStack", "u": "ros2-messages.html", "x": "ExoStack uses only standard message types, so no interface package has to be built, not even for micro-ROS. The meaning therefore lies in the layout : which element of a message carries what. The same Float32MultiArray carries a profile on one topic and a cost on another. Each layout below names its standard type, linked to the official definition, and lists every element. Hub message : LaserScan Sync trigger : Int32 Synchronized channels : Float32MultiArray Gait event : Int32 Estimator inputs : Float32 Assistance profile : Float32MultiArray Actuator set-point : Float32 Profile status : Float32MultiArray Manual override : Int32 Cost : Float32MultiArray HIL candidate : Float32MultiArray External sensors : Float32MultiArray Additional gait marker : Int32"}, {"t": "Hub message", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-hub", "x": "Type sensor_msgs/msg/LaserScan Topic /<hub topic> , e.g. /hub_right From → to ESA sensing hub → synchronizer One message carries every channel of a board, so the hubs need no custom message type. ESA sets four fields; the others keep their defaults. Element Type Unit Meaning header.stamp builtin_interfaces/Time s, ns hub time since the last /sync_trigger : millis() minus the offset stored on the trigger header.frame_id string n/a not set (empty) range_min float32 count number of devices in deviceList[] range_max float32 count number of channels, i.e. the length of intensities intensities[i] float32[] per device channel i in device-list order: AI in volts (0–3.3; a battery channel ×1.34, at most 4.2), DI 0 or 1, I²C in the driver's units (e.g. N for the NAU7802, degrees for BNO085 angles) angle_*, time_increment, scan_time, ranges float32, float32[] n/a not used (0, empty) /hub_right: worked example of the manual (values illustrative) header: stamp: {sec: 12, nanosec: 340000000} # 12.34 s since the last /sync_trigger frame_id: '' range_min: 5.0 # devices: 2 IMUs, 2 load cells, 1 FSR range_max: 21.0 # channels intensities: [4.1, -12.6, 87.0, ...] # 21 values, in device-list order"}, {"t": "Sync trigger", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-sync-trigger", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Int32 Topic /sync_trigger (fixed name) From → to synchronizer → every hub A time reference rather than data: the value is not read. Element Type Unit Meaning data int32 n/a always 0. On reception a hub stores millis() as its offset, so its later stamps count from this trigger sent at 5 Hz while the synchronizer is in trigger mode data: 0"}, {"t": "Synchronized channels", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-synced-sensors", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32MultiArray Topic /synced_sensors ( output_topic of sensors.yaml ) From → to synchronizer → detectors, cost evaluators The channels of every hub listed in sensors.yaml , concatenated hub by hub in the file's order, each hub's channels in its sensors order. Algorithms never index this array: they ask for [hub, channel] pairs and SensorUtil finds the index. Element Type Unit Meaning layout std_msgs/MultiArrayLayout n/a not used (empty) data[i] float32[] as published by the hub channel i of the concatenation; no time stamp is included Index map with the shipped sensors.yaml Two hubs, hubl on /hub_left and hubr on /hub_right , publish the same 21 channels, so /synced_sensors carries 42 values. index (hubl) index (hubr) channel 0 21 thigh_roll 1 22 thigh_pitch 2 23 thigh_yaw 3 24 thigh_omega_x 4 25 thigh_omega_y 5 26 thigh_omega_z 6 27 thigh_acc_x 7 28 thigh_acc_y 8 29 thigh_acc_z 9 30 shank_roll 10 31 shank_pitch 11 32 shank_yaw 12 33 shank_omega_x 13 34 shank_omega_y 14 35 shank_omega_z 15 36 shank_acc_x 16 37 shank_acc_y 17 38 shank_acc_z 18 39 force_flexion 19 40 force_extension 20 41 fsr with the shipped sensors.yaml data: [3.2, -1.4, 0.6, ..., 41.8, 0.0, 2.9] # 42 values"}, {"t": "Gait event", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-event", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Int32 Topic /<event topic> , e.g. /heel_strike_right From → to detector → planner A pulse: one message per detected event, nothing in between. Element Type Unit Meaning data int32 n/a 1 = event detected. The planner reacts only to 1 one message per heel strike data: 1"}, {"t": "Estimator inputs", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-estimator", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32 Topic /<speed_topic> , /<intent_topic> , /<gait_phase_topic> From → to your estimator → profile node or planner Three scalar inputs share this layout; their meaning comes from the topic they are sent on. Element Type Unit Meaning data on speed_topic float32 unit of the table keys (e.g. km/h) walking speed; the lookup plug-in picks or interpolates the table row for it data on intent_topic float32 label locomotion intent: the key of an intent table in Lookup_Speed_Intent_Segmentwise ; 0 = standing, which raises the profile's stop flag data on gait_phase_topic float32 unit of the profile's t gait phase, used directly as the time argument t of a gait_phase_based planner /walking_speed data: 3.5"}, {"t": "Assistance profile", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-profile", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32MultiArray Topic /<profile_topic> , e.g. /profile/right_knee_flexion From → to profile node → planner The profile the planner plays after the next gait event. Its first and last elements frame what the builder produced. Element Type Unit Meaning data[0] float32 n/a k , the candidate number: 0 in Deployment, 1, 2, … in HIL data[1] float32 n/a type : 1 = list of waypoints, 2 = polynomial coefficients data[2] float32 t units start of the profile window data[3 … n−3] float32 t units, set-point units body : (t, value) pairs for type 1, coefficients for type 2 data[n−2] float32 t units end of the profile window data[n−1] float32 n/a stop : 1 = emergency stop (intent 0), else 0 What the builders put in the body List builders add the default value 20 at the start and end of the window. The input is a lookup-table row in Deployment or the candidate's parameters in HIL. Builder type input body Builder1_List 1 [start, peak_t, peak_v, end] (start, 20) (peak_t, peak_v) (end, 20) Builder2_List 1 [start, t1, v1, t2, v2, end] (start, 20) (t1, v1) (t2, v2) (end, 20) Builder_Nwaypoint_List 1 [start, t1, v1, …, tN, vN, end] (start, 20) (t1, v1) … (tN, vN) (end, 20) Builder1_Function 2 [start, c0, c1, …, cn, end] c0 … cn In v1.0, type-2 profiles do not run (see Known limitations ). Deployment, Builder2_List, table row [50, 200, 500, 600, 500, 800] data: [0, 1, 50, 50, 20, 200, 500, 600, 500, 800, 20, 800, 0] # k = 0 (Deployment), type = 1 (list), start = 50 # waypoints (t, value): (50, 20) (200, 500) (600, 500) (800, 20) # end = 800, stop = 0"}, {"t": "Actuator set-point", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-command", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32 Topic /<output_topic> = the actuator's command topic, e.g. /right/hip_ppr_cmd From → to planner → actuation hub or any ROS 2 driver The only message an actuator receives. The actuation hub keeps the latest value per actuator and applies it in every actuate() . Element Type Unit Meaning data float32 driver unit set-point: kPa for ACT_FESTO_VEAB_L26 , degrees for ACT_SG90_SERVO ; 0 until the first message pressure set-point in kPa data: 412.5"}, {"t": "Profile status", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-profile-status", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32MultiArray Topic /<planner>/profile_status From → to planner → cost evaluator Sent at every gait event, it tells the cost evaluator which candidate is being played and how many cycles of it have passed. Element Type Unit Meaning data[0] float32 n/a k of the active profile (0 in Deployment) data[1] float32 cycles cycle : gait cycles since the candidate became active; counts only in HIL, 0 in Deployment /right_knee_flexion/profile_status data: [3.0, 4.0] # candidate 3, fourth gait cycle"}, {"t": "Manual override", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-manual", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Int32 Topic /<planner>/manual_mode (Int32) and /<planner>/manual_cmd (Float32) From → to PASE or you → planner Two topics per planner let you drive the actuator by hand, e.g. to test it. The command shares the Float32 set-point layout. Element Type Unit Meaning manual_mode: data int32 n/a 1 = manual override on, 0 = back to the profiles manual_cmd: data float32 driver unit set-point sent on output_topic while manual mode is on terminal ros2 topic pub --once /right_hip_extension/manual_mode std_msgs/msg/Int32 \"{data: 1}\" ros2 topic pub --once /right_hip_extension/manual_cmd std_msgs/msg/Float32 \"{data: 150.0}\""}, {"t": "Cost", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-cost", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32MultiArray Topic /<cost_topic> , e.g. /cost1 From → to cost evaluator → HIL optimizer One message per evaluated candidate. Element Type Unit Meaning data[0] float32 n/a k of the candidate the cost belongs to (0 in Deployment) data[1] float32 cost-plug-in unit cost : mean of the per-cycle costs over gait_cycles_to_consider cycles, after skipping gait_cycles_to_skip /cost1 data: [3.0, 27.4]"}, {"t": "HIL candidate", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-hil-output", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32MultiArray Topic /<hil_output_topic> , e.g. /hil_output1 From → to HIL optimizer → profile node The next parameter set to try. Its parameters are the builder's inputs, in the order of mean and bounds in hil_hyperparams . Element Type Unit Meaning data[0] float32 n/a k , the candidate number, increasing from 1 data[1 … n] float32 builder-input units p₁ … pₙ , one value per optimized dimension profile1 of the shipped profile.yaml: six dimensions data: [3, 50, 200, 200, 400, 200, 700] # -> profile node (Builder2_List): [3, 1, 50, 50, 20, 200, 200, 400, 200, 700, 20, 700, 0]"}, {"t": "External sensors", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-external", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Float32MultiArray Topic /<external_sensors_topic> From → to your sensor node → cost evaluator Signals that are not on a hub, e.g. a metabolic system or an EMG amplifier, reach the cost plug-in through this topic. Element Type Unit Meaning data[i] float32[] your units any vector of fixed length; the cost plug-in receives it as one sample row illustrative data: [312.0, 0.84] # e.g. metabolic power (W), respiratory quotient"}, {"t": "Additional gait marker", "p": "Message layouts", "u": "ros2-messages.html#msg-gait-marker", "x": "Type std_msgs/msg/Int32 Topic /<additional_gait_marker_topic> From → to your node → cost evaluator A marker for events the cost plug-in needs inside a cycle, e.g. the flexion–extension split. Element Type Unit Meaning data int32 n/a marker value, appended to the next cost sample and then reset to 0 illustrative data: 1"}];